A||||26||bandit1||2.8 V6 TO 3.1 or 3.4???
Z||000000||bandit1||08-01-99||10:10 PM||||My 2.8 V6 is using alot of oil.  It just drinks it i swear, and its bogging out so bad im going to tear out the engine.  I have connections to a salvage yard that has plenty of 3.1, 3.4 3.8, anything!!  What engine would be the cheapest to put in, and the least amount of headache.  And how hard is it to move the starter to the other side??  Would any of these engines bolt up to a 3 speed auto?  (oh yea, i need an engine w/alittle bit of power, at least power enough to burn em off.)  Thanks
Z||000001||batboy||08-01-99||11:30 PM||||The 3.1 and the 3.4 will both physically bolt to the Fiero transmission because they are in the 60 degree V6 family like the 2.8 liter. The 3.8 will not, if you mean the 3800, it will not either. Ed Parks is the man to talk to about swapping a 3.4 in. The 3.1 with cast iron heads (like out of early 90's minivans) is a fairly easy swap (especially if you use all the 2.8 externals like pulleys etc.). Other FWD 3.1 engines with aluminum heads will work too, but you must use the donor vehicle's ECM and engine wiring harness since the fuel injection is different. Another possiblity is rebuilding the 2.8 into a 3.1 (3.2 if you bore it out). This is done by using the 3.1 crank, pistons, and flywheel. Anything other engine swap requires a lot of work and special conversion kits. Good luck.
Z||000002||bandit1||08-02-99||09:53 AM||||so batboy, your saying that if i find a 90's minivan w/ a 3.1 cast iron heads, that i should have no problem putting it in?  Im not looking to rebuild this 2.8, i find it to be a POS.  Would there be a noticible difference between a 3.1 and a 2.8?  And what things will i need to salvage from my 2.8?
Z||000003||bandit1||08-02-99||10:11 AM||||Also, i need to know how much each of these would cost??  I was reading on of the topics about 3.8 and how fuel economy is so great.  Im going to get the engine and tranny for free for any engine i get, its just the labor of putting it in, thats the only cost.  Im really interested in the 3.8 but i dont what to spend much money for a conversion kit, and i would rather have a engine that would match my 3 speed auto.  When i saw that the 3.8 produced 35 mpg, i about crapped.  My 2.8 gives me about 15!! and its slow as hell!! always has been!!  I need a new engine now!  Any ideas on price?
Z||000004||Phil||08-02-99||10:31 AM||||Just to install a 3800 engine is going to cost on the plus side of $2000. To install another 2.8 cost is $0 just time. Your call
Z||000005||bandit1||08-02-99||03:26 PM||||Is it true that you have to pull the engine from underneath????
Z||000006||batboy||08-02-99||08:07 PM||||Bandit, I'll try to answer your questions the best I can.<p>1) The cast iron 3.1 liter engines from the early 90's front-wheel drive GM minivans will bolt right up to a Fiero and use all the existing Fiero engine accessories and parts. About the only thing additional is that you need to use an adjustable fuel pressure regulator and a neutrally balanced flywheel or flexplate. The cool thing about using the manifold and fuel injection from the Fiero is that you don't have to change wiring or computers, AND it looks like a factory 2.8 sitting in there *grin*.<p>2)  How much power gain will the stock 3.1 have over the stock 2.8? I won't lie to you, not a huge difference. It does increase torque more than horsepower and more low end torque is good if this is a street engine. But if you add headers and a good flowing exhaust at the same time you do the swap, now you're starting to get a noticeable power increase.<p>3)  The mid 30's gas mileage of a 3800 V6 swap into a Fiero was with using the matching 4 speed automatic overdrive and highway driving. But, like they always say, "Your mileage my vary!"<p>4)  Phil is probably pretty close to the cost when he said figure $2,000 plus for the conversion alone. That expense is what's stopping me from doing that swap right now. Plus it's quite a bit of work to do the swap. You must rewire the engine compartment and use the 3800 tranny and computer too.<p>5)  I do not have personal experience in pulling a Fiero engine from the top, but I've heard it can be done. However, the common way is to drop the whole engine, transmission, and cradle out from below. Shaun's web site has instructions on how to pull the engine from the top. Go to the below listed URL:<p>http://members.aol.com/shaun41178/Page3.html#Removing Engine From the Top
Z||000007||squid||08-02-99||10:11 PM||||I have an '85 4spd 2.8 car that I rebuilt with a 3.1L crank from a '90 Pontiac 6000 and new pistons for a '90 firebird with a 3.1/cast iron head. I cant say that I can tell much of a difference with it but since I used factory parts it didnt cost much more than a stock rebuild if you had a bad crank. I'm gathering parts for a 4.3 swap in my '87 right now. That should be plenty.
Z||000008||WisconsinGT||08-03-99||04:15 PM||||WOW! I am at a point right this minute where I am going to rebuild my 2.8 heads. But now I am thinking of getting 3.1 heads. Is it really a direct bolt-on? What about the exhaust manafold...Is is a bolt and go process as well? I would really like to know the in's and out's of putting 3.1 heads on my 2.8 engine. Also, is it only the mini vans that have the heads? What about early cameros and the like.<p>Thanks
Z||000009||batboy||08-03-99||06:52 PM||||The heads! This is a tricky subject which has been discussed in a couple other threads in the past. First off, I wouldn't be so quick to toss the Fiero 2.8 heads off to the side. Those are HO heads with larger valves than the standard 2.8 cast iron heads. I don't know if the cast iron 3.1 heads are the small valve ones or the HO ones, but any of the cast iron 60 degree V6 family of heads will bolt on without any problems. The intake and exhaust manifolds bolt on too. However, most 3.1 FWD cars used the newer style (Generation II) aluminum heads (not sure about the Camaros), which flow great and make great power, BUT... you can't use the older style Fiero intake on them. You are stuck using the intake and fuel injection from the donor car, along with the engine wiring harness and computer too. Nothing is ever simple is it?
Z||000010||WisconsinGT||08-03-99||08:01 PM||||Cheers Batboy! I guess I was looking at two different web sites. It is the 3.4L heads that I was wondering about. Here is the web site: http://members.aol.com/NitrousV6/Adams1.html. It has some great info on mods for the Fiero. Apparently, the 3.4L (Cast iron) heads are the best way to go. But, you have a good point about staying with the 2.8 heads. I guess it is a toss up at this point. Anyone have info. on the 3.4L heads? Are you happy with the performance/economy? Is it a true transparent swap?
Z||000011||batboy||08-03-99||09:38 PM||||Hey WisconsinGT, I guess you can learn something new everyday. I looked at that URL about the Camaro 3.4 heads and then did some checking around. Sure enough, at least on some of the high performance 3.4 heads, they have a larger exhaust valve than the 2.8 HO Fiero heads (intake valves are the same). The Sallee Chevrolet site confirmed this.<p>Standard 2.8 cast iron heads: intake valves = 1.60" and exhaust valves = 1.30"<p>2.8 HO cast iron heads: intake valves = 1.72" and exhaust valve = 1.42"<p>3.4 high performance heads: intake valves = 1.72" and exhaust valves = 1.60"<p>That web site you listed claimed that the larger exhaust valves were good for an additional 10 extra ponies. I ran all the figures through my Desktop Dyno computer program (which I have found to be amazingly accurate) and guess what? I came up with nearly a 10 horsepower gain with the larger exhaust valves as the only variable I changed over the HO 2.8 heads. At least another 12 ponies or more if you do some pocket porting to the heads.<p>As far as I can tell, these heads are a total bolt on swap, almost sounds too good to be true, huh. Thank the performance gods for Camaros I guess. Get one of those 3.1 engines from a mini-van, add those 3.4 heads, a hi-po cam, headers, a few other nifty goodies, and suddenly you have a Fiero swap that is easy, affordable, and more powerful than the stock 2.8 liter engine. I bet a good machine shop could add the bigger exhaust valves to the Fiero HO heads, but I'd do a little research into that beforehand.
Z||000012||Shaun41178||08-05-99||01:23 AM||||The website that you are talking about is mine.  i am kind of flattered to see it being posted here on the message board.<p>I pretty much took a guess as to how much power those heads would add.  Looks to me like it was a good guess.<p>However just don't go out and buy heads off of a 3.4 camaor or whatever.  i have also heard a story that not all 3.4's have the 1.6 exhasut valve.  So before purchasing check the valve diameter first.  If it is a 1.42 then it is the same head as a stock Fiero head.
Z||000013||PaUL||08-05-99||04:58 PM||||Actually you could have the stock fiero or any 60deg v6 heads reground for larger valves.
Z||000014||Eric||08-05-99||05:21 PM||||Yeah, that's what my machinist said.  There's not a whole lot of room to work with, but he should be able to size the valves as big as or bigger than the 3.4.  <p>Batboy, is there more performance gain from oversizing the exaust valves, intake valves or both?
Z||000015||PaUL||08-05-99||06:20 PM||||You'll just need to source the valves if you are going to have your heads recut for larger valves. I've only seen them listed in one catalog, PAW, made by Manley. They are available in 2 versions, one for cast iron heads the other aluminum, listed under the 3.1 v6. I'm assuming all years for the cast iron heads had the same valve stem length whether it be the 2.8, 2.8 HO, 3.1, or 3.4. Is this true?
Z||000016||batboy||08-05-99||06:57 PM||||Ok guys, I'll be happy to crunch some numbers for you, but like PaUL says, you have to find the right valves first. Length and stem diameter will be critical. I'm not 100% sure, but I think you need an overall length of 4.705 with a stem diameter of 11/32 on the intakes and a length of 4.736 with a stem diameter of 11/32 on the exhaust valves. I could not find any larger intake valves to match these specs, but I ran the program anyway using an intake head diameter of 1.84" just for kicks.<p>I plugged in numbers for a 3.1 liter with 9:1 compression ratio, GM HO cam, and headers. With the Fiero HO heads using the original valve sizes, horsepower was about 200 @ 5,500 rpm and torque was about 220 @ 4,000 rpm. The bigger (1.60") exhaust valves gave about 10 extra ponies and almost no extra torque. The 1.84" intakes increased horsepower by maybe 4 or 5, with or without using the larger exhaust valves. Torque increased about 3 ft/lbs.<p>There is probably a reason why the factory didn't go with larger intake valves, probably because the combustion chamber design shrouds larger intake valves thus negating any possible power gains (not to mention the lack of available intake valves that size) or that the metal gets too thin there. If I were doing it, I'd forget about installing larger intakes and go with just putting in the larger exhaust valves. You get more bang for your buck with the exhaust valves.
Z||000017||PaUL||08-05-99||07:22 PM||||I would think they had to take gas mileange into consideration when they chose the valves for the various engines. They have to meet the CAFE requirments afterall. <p>Batboy, your program sounds interesting. Is there a point of dimishing returns as you increase the size of the valves? Will having too large a valve actually decrease the power? Can the program deal with multi valve engines like all the imports have? What are the advantages of having 4 smaller valves instead of 2 big ones anyway? 
Z||000018||batboy||08-05-99||07:58 PM||||PaUL, you have to use a little common sense and logic when playing around with valve sizes. The program assumes that if you put "X" size valves in the head that the head will respond to that size valve. In reality, that's not always true. For example, although it's physically possible to put 2.05" intakes into a run-of-the-mill stock small block head, a true dyno test usually reveals that anything larger than 2.02" valve does not gain anything and often (depending on the head casting) a 2.02" valve doesn't do much either without the proper porting. Yes, my program can handle 4 valve per cylinder head designs. Although, I must admit I don't know much about that type of head or why they flow better.
Z||000019||Eric||08-05-99||08:14 PM||||Thanks Batboy.  I'll tell my machinist to put the larger exaust valves in.  It's good to have the confimation of the program and the fact that GM already did this to the HO heads before I throw a bunch of cash around.
Z||000020||batboy||08-05-99||09:08 PM||||Eric, my feelings and thoughts too. Keep us posted on the results. I wonder if you called Chevrolet, whether they would tell you if those 3.4 heads had more meat (more metal) than the HO 2.8 Fiero heads or if they were the same basic casting. I'd hate for you to hit the water jacket and ruin a good set of heads. Also, if you have the heads machine for bigger exhaust valves, you ought to spend a few extra bucks and have the harden exhaust seats installed too. This is needed because the unleaded fuel burns up the seats quicker than the old leaded gas. Everytime I have an old set of heads rebuilt, I have my machine shop do this.
Z||000021||WisconsinGT||08-05-99||11:05 PM||||Well, this thread is getting interesting. So. I guess it is safe to say that no one has performed the install of the heads on the original 2.8 Fiero engine. Eric, good luck. I think I wil stay with my original heads. (Unless they are cracked.) I just think it would be safer to work with the original heads. No? Anyway, let us know the results Eric.
Z||000022||WisconsinGT||08-05-99||11:09 PM||||oh yeah! I forgot to metion...I spoke with Ed Parks. He said that you have to change a sensor (I think) from the right side to the left. 
